{"source_key":"sha256-db92dd9d54884ccb80de5ab4e2691d5a","record_index":311,"record_hash":"sha256:7fb6a250a4c4263cd60aa54edce5cebe7c5eaa54fd5beb2a010ce1046ee6ed9a","timestamp":"2026-07-12T13:23:09.899Z","kind":"message","source_event_kind":"codex:response_item:message","role":"assistant","text":"Yes—the live removal and snapshot compatibility can happen in one change. Snapshot compatibility does not require maintaining a live mirror.\n\nThe clean one-step design is:\n\n1. `ProjectMeta` becomes the only live source of truth in Git config.\n2. Live `virtual_branches.toml` stops serializing and consuming `[default_target]`.\n3. New oplog snapshots add authoritative `project_meta.toml`.\n4. When building a snapshot, GitButler injects a synthesized `[default_target]` only into the snapshot’s copy of `virtual_branches.toml`.\n5. Restore prefers `project_meta.toml`; old snapshots fall back to their legacy target. The compatibility target is stripped before writing live TOML.\n6. Keep oplog version 3, `portedMeta=true`, and the existing SQLite columns as inert schema compatibility. Move refresh-sentinel notification to the ProjectMeta write path.\n\nThat eliminates split brain: the legacy representation exists only in immutable compatibility artifacts, never as live state.\n\nThe limitation is downgrade scope:\n\n- A ProjectMeta-aware older release can open the project and restore snapshots made by the new release.\n- But after downgrading, that older release may create its own snapshot from the target-free live TOML. Its old restore code can then mishandle that snapshot.\n- Pre-ProjectMeta releases cannot reliably open the project at all.\n\nSo there are two honest choices:\n\n- Recommended given your priority: do the one-step removal and support full operation from this release forward. Older ProjectMeta-aware versions are best-effort downgrade targets, not fully supported for continued oplog use.\n- If “downgrade” must include fully safe undo/redo after returning to the previous release, a bridge release remains unavoidable.\n\nI would take the first option. It removes the dual-write, the legacy live projection, and the split-brain repair machinery immediately, while retaining old-snapshot recovery through a very small oplog-only adapter. A one-time input parser can still migrate dormant projects and then discard the legacy section; it never becomes a second authority.","tool_name":null,"tool_input":null,"source_record":{"timestamp":"2026-07-12T13:23:09.899Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","role":"assistant","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"[REDACTED:entropy]"},"phase":"final_answer"}}}